==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{m|en|aldehyde}} {{suffix|en||anine}} in reference to [[aldehyde]], with the [[infix]] [[-an-]] for ease of pronunciation, when the German chemist [[w:Adolph Strecker|Adolph Strecker]] first synthesized alanine in 1850 by mixing [[acetaldehyde]] (then just known as aldehyde) with [[ammonia]], [[hydrogen cyanide]], and [[hydrochloric acid]].

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|amino acid|uncountable}} A [[nonessential amino acid]] ''2-aminopropanoic acid'' found in most [[animal]] [[protein]]s
#: {{ux|en|Potatoes can be a good source of '''alanine'''.}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} A specific [[residue]], [[molecule]], or [[isomer]] of this amino acid
#: {{ux|en|Two '''alanines''' are replaced by prolines.}}

